is the same. The **data density** (bitrate) is vastly different. Here is a more technical way to visualize it: Imagine a 1080p screen is a grid of 2,073,600 tiny lights (pixels). - **YouTube's job:** Change the color of those lights 60 times per second, but only change the ones that *need* to change, and describe those changes in the most efficient way possible. "Light #1,000,000 stays the same. Light #1,000,001 changes from blue to a slightly darker blue, but I can describe that using a math formula instead of a full color value." - **Stadia's job:** Change the color of *every single light* 60 times per second, because the entire scene is being rendered from scratch. It has to describe the new color for every single pixel, every single frame, and it has to do it so fast that it can't use the fancy math formulas. **In short: "1080p" is just the frame. The "data" is the paint used to fill it.** YouTube is a master of using a tiny drop of paint to cover a huge area. Stadia has to slather on a thick coat of paint every single frame to keep the picture looking sharp and responsive.
