22:40:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very sharp observation, and you're right to be skeptical. If they both *look* like 1080p to you, it's natural to wonder why the data usage is so different. The key is that **"looking like 1080p" is not the same as "having the same visual quality."** The "1080p" label only guarantees the *number of pixels*, not the *quality of those pixels*. Think of it like two photographs of the same scene, both printed on the same size paper: - **Photo A (YouTube):** A sharp, clean, professional photo. - **Photo B (Stadia):** The same photo, but printed with a cheap, low-quality printer. Both are the same size (resolution). But if you look very closely at Photo B, you'll see: - **Blocky artifacts:** The image is made of visible little squares. - **Blurry details:** Fine textures (like grass or hair) look smeared or mushy. - **Color banding:** Smooth gradients (like a sunset sky) look like a series of flat, distinct stripes. - **"Mosquito noise":** A shimmering, fuzzy halo around sharp edges. This is the result of **low bitrate compression**. The image is "pixelated" or "compressed" to save data. Now, here's the crucial part: **You might not notice these flaws on a typical screen or from a typical viewing distance.** - **YouTube's 1080p** is designed to look *very good* at a low bitrate because it uses the best possible compression on clean, predictable video. - **Stadia's 1080p** is fighting a losing battle. To keep the game responsive, it uses a less efficient, faster compression. To hide the resulting artifacts, it has to use a **much higher bitrate** than YouTube. If Stadia used YouTube's bitrate, the game would look like a blurry, blocky, artifact-ridden mess. So, when you watch a YouTube video at the same resolution, you are seeing a **much cleaner, more detailed image** because the compression is far more efficient. Stadia is using more data just to achieve an *acceptable* level of quality that doesn't look terrible. **Let's use a specific example:** Imagine a 1080p YouTube video of a fast-moving car race. The encoder has time to analyze the whole clip. It can say, "The background is a blur, so I'll compress that heavily. The car is sharp, so I'll give it more data." The result is a clean, sharp car against a blurry background. Now imagine playing a racing game on Stadia. The encoder has to compress a *new* frame every 16ms. It can't plan. It sees a sharp car, a sharp background, and a sharp track. To keep *all* of it from looking blocky, it has to throw a lot of data at every single frame. The result is a decent-looking car, but maybe the background has some shimmering or the track has some blocky patches. **The bottom line:** You are correct that both are labeled "1080p." But the **visual quality is not the same.** YouTube's 1080p is a high-quality, efficiently compressed image. Stadia's 1080p is a lower-quality, less-efficiently compressed image that requires more data to avoid looking terrible. The "1080p" label is a simplification that hides this crucial difference in quality and data usage.
