22:42:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a very logical question, and it gets to the heart of the matter. The answer is **no, they would not look the same**, even though both are 1080p. Let me explain why, using a very simple visual example you can try right now. **The "JPG vs. PNG" Analogy (A Real-World Example)** Think about two photos on your phone. Both are the same size (say, 4 inches wide). But one is saved as a **JPG** (a compressed format) and one is saved as a **PNG** (a less compressed format). - If you zoom in *really close* on the JPG, you'll see little blocky squares, especially around edges or in areas of solid color. The picture looks a bit "crunchy" or "pixelated." - If you zoom in on the PNG, the edges are smooth and clean. **Both photos are the same size (same resolution).** But they look different because the *data* inside them is different. The JPG threw away some detail to make the file smaller. The PNG kept all the detail. **This is exactly what's happening with YouTube vs. Stadia.** - **YouTube's 1080p** is like a high-quality PNG. The encoder spent a lot of time making sure every pixel is described as accurately as possible, using very little data. - **Stadia's 1080p** is like a low-quality JPG. The encoder had to work very fast and couldn't be as precise. To keep the picture from looking like a total mess, it has to use a lot more data. **The result on your screen:** If you put them side-by-side, you would see that the YouTube video is sharper, has cleaner edges, and handles motion more smoothly. The Stadia stream would look slightly softer, have more "blocky" artifacts in fast-moving scenes, and might have a bit of a "shimmer" or "fuzziness" around objects. **So, to answer your question directly:** No, they would not look the same. The resolution is the same, but the **quality of the picture** is different because the compression methods are different. Your screen faithfully displays whatever data it receives. It doesn't "fix" the compression artifacts. It shows them exactly as they are. **The "1080p" label is a promise about the *number* of pixels. It is not a promise about the *fidelity* of those pixels.**
